theory.intro:
Rotating a circular arrangement does not create a new one, because the objects keep the same clockwise relative order. A reflection reverses that order and is a different arrangement at a table. Fixing one object's position removes the $n$ rotations, leaving $(n-1)!$ arrangements of $n$ distinct objects. If the circle can also be flipped over, divide by $2$ when there are at least $3$ distinct objects; with $1$ or $2$ distinct objects, there is $1$ arrangement.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0,0) circle (2);
\fill (90:2) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (30:2) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (-30:2) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (-90:2) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (-150:2) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (150:2) circle (2.5pt);
\node at (90:2.75) {$A$ (fixed)};
\node at (30:2.7) {$1$};
\node at (-30:2.7) {$2$};
\node at (-90:2.7) {$3$};
\node at (-150:2.7) {$\cdots$};
\node at (150:2.85) {$n-1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

facts:
A keyring, bracelet or necklace can be flipped over, so a reflection is the same arrangement. For at least $3$ distinct objects, the count is $\dfrac{(n-1)!}{2}$; with $1$ or $2$ distinct objects, the count is $1$.

